WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.960
Digging for the truth is the name of the&nbsp;
game when it comes to True Detective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.520
but here are some secrets that even the most&nbsp;
diehard fans may not have uncovered yet.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.580
Matthew McConaughey took his role&nbsp;
as one of the leading stars and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.700
executive producers of the first&nbsp;
season of HBO's True Detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.320
seriously. Following McConaughey's powerful&nbsp;
performance in 2011's The Lincoln Lawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.120
show creator Nic Pizzolatto saw the potential for&nbsp;
McConaughey's involvement in the series as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.181
character Rustin Cohle, acting alongside&nbsp;
Woody Harrelson and Michelle Monaghan.

00:00:28.181 --> 00:00:35.884
"I mean, it's like something's just got your name&nbsp;
on it, like a bullet, or a nail in the road."

00:00:35.884 --> 00:00:40.740
Cohle is a complex character who could easily&nbsp;
alienate an audience with his nihilism and apathy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.280
so it was essential for McConaughey&nbsp;
to fully embody the deep pain of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.200
character. He arguably managed to do so by&nbsp;
extensively analyzing Cohle's identity to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.120
fundamental level. According to an interview&nbsp;
with Rolling Stone back in 2014, McConaughey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.860
explained his need to create a document to chart&nbsp;
his character's life and growth over the 17 year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:06.780
timeline depicted in Season 1. He broke Cohle's&nbsp;
essence down into four stages: 1995, Crash, 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.180
and 2012. Each section contained its own lengthy&nbsp;
analysis of the character's state of mind at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.658
each time period. The result was a 450-page&nbsp;
graph titled Four Stages of Rustin Cohle.

00:01:17.658 --> 00:01:22.260
It's common for many television shows not&nbsp;
to have multi-season arcs planned out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.180
the very beginning and — as a result — they can&nbsp;
lose direction. True Detective didn't have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.620
problem, as Pizzolatto always intended the&nbsp;
show to be an anthology with new casts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.223
stories every season, much like Ryan Murphy and&nbsp;
Brad Falchuk's FX series American Horror Story.

00:01:37.223 --> 00:01:42.000
The strength of an anthology format comes from&nbsp;
creators exploring vastly different stories while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.840
maintaining an overarching theme and tone.&nbsp;
While the characters and setting change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.880
each season follows the same basic format: two&nbsp;
detectives investigating crimes that lead to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.500
discovery of some wider conspiracy or mystery.&nbsp;
The second season cast Colin Farrell and Rachel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.600
McAdams as their duo — with a notable performance&nbsp;
from Vince Vaughn as a career criminal — while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.400
the third season had Mahershala Ali and Stephen&nbsp;
Dorff. However, in an interview with Entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.840
Weekly, Pizzolatto claimed that he initially&nbsp;
began writing the story that eventually became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.824
Season 3 as a new project, only to realize&nbsp;
that it fit the themes of True Detective.

00:02:14.824 --> 00:02:17.160
"I can understand your&nbsp;
frustration with the disease."

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.020
"A disease you can't even say what it is."

00:02:19.902 --> 00:02:21.942
"We can say what it almost certainly is."

00:02:21.942 --> 00:02:26.640
But the anthology format doesn't sit well&nbsp;
with all fans, with some missing the chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.680
between Harrelson and McConaughey, and Season 2&nbsp;
overall receiving lukewarm critical and audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.600
receptions. Although most fans and critics agree&nbsp;
that Season 3 was something of a return to form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.440
no matter how each season fares on their&nbsp;
own merits, the deliberate variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.000
provides fresh interest while preserving&nbsp;
Pizzolatto's dark, atmospheric style.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.680
It hasn't always been smooth sailing for True&nbsp;
Detective. While the first season was hailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.180
as a towering achievement of episodic television,&nbsp;
the second and third seasons struggled to live up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.660
to the hype. But one of the more troubling&nbsp;
developments for the show occurred during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.040
production of the second season after&nbsp;
the departure of Cary Joji Fukunaga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.620
the first season's director. The hole left by this&nbsp;
major change forced HBO and Pizzolatto to search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.060
for other artists to direct episodes throughout&nbsp;
the next season, and ended up splitting the load&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.760
between six different directors, which included&nbsp;
Justin Lin, Daniel Attias, and Miguel Sapochnik.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.240
Some even bigger names were approached&nbsp;
early on but, for one reason or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.500
they declined the offer to helm an episode.&nbsp;
The biggest of these filmmakers was horror icon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.663
David Cronenberg, best known for creating classic&nbsp;
films such as Scanners, The Fly, and Videodrome.

00:03:31.663 --> 00:03:35.700
According to Vulture, Cronenberg was&nbsp;
approached to direct, but refused, saying,

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.240
"...I thought that the script was&nbsp;
bad, so I didn't do it. In TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.100
the director is just a traffic cop, but on the&nbsp;
other hand it is work and there's a lot of it."

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.080
What began as a working relationship between two&nbsp;
actors has reportedly grown into a deeply genuine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.640
friendship between McConaughey and Harrelson.&nbsp;
The lead actors and executive producers on True&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.720
Detective played two diametrically opposing&nbsp;
partners who slowly grew to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.460
respect, and maybe even like each other&nbsp;
as the story unfolds. This combative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.180
relationship between Rust Cohle and Marty&nbsp;
Hart was apparently difficult for them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.962
find when filming began because&nbsp;
of their existing relationship.

00:04:08.962 --> 00:04:12.322
"Yeah, I didn't even know I'd hit him&nbsp;
the first time. He was like 'yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.960
good one. Yeah, nice one.'"

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:16.901
"Let me get this."

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:20.760
True Detective was not the first time these&nbsp;
two actors worked together, with them sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.181
screen time in the lighthearted comedies EDtv&nbsp;
and Surfer, Dude. According to USA Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.181 --> 00:04:30.300
McConaughey and Harrelson had to focus on&nbsp;
breaking their existing off-screen dynamic so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.080
that their characters could be appropriately&nbsp;
hostile to one another. Harrelson claimed,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.724
"Not being on the same wavelength aided what it&nbsp;
was we were doing, but it was also uncomfortable."

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.540
True Detective doesn't shy away from&nbsp;
the grimmest aspects of true crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:46.260
with every season's underlying themes&nbsp;
encompassing almost an otherworldly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.240
evil. Season 1 heavily references aspects of&nbsp;
occultism behind the murders at the show's center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.380
with creator Nic Pizzolatto directly&nbsp;
referencing horror author Robert W.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.760
Chambers' book The King in Yellow to create&nbsp;
the series' eerie atmosphere and plot.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.100
Some key terms and phrases from this relatively&nbsp;
obscure piece of 19th-century supernatural horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.360
are peppered throughout the first season, with&nbsp;
the unknown killer Rust and Marty hunts known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.980
the Yellow King, who lives in a dark place called&nbsp;
Carcosa. These are lifted directly from the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.320
about the evil entity known as The King in Yellow,&nbsp;
as well as a lost city named Carcosa — itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.960
borrowed from an even older short story by seminal&nbsp;
American author Ambrose Bierce. But Chambers was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.520
also a strong admirer of H.P. Lovecraft, so&nbsp;
his work was heavily infused with a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.900
sense of cosmic dread. This, in turn, inspired&nbsp;
Pizzolatto during his series' development.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.820
In an interview with EW, Pizzolatto directly&nbsp;
addressed how the horror elements of Chambers'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.442
book changed his writing process, claiming,

00:05:41.442 --> 00:05:45.102
"When I did that, a kind of secondary&nbsp;
language began to form in the scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.102 --> 00:05:48.804
where the notion of cosmic horror became&nbsp;
a very real part of the environment."

00:05:48.804 --> 00:05:52.800
While most television shows and movies are&nbsp;
filmed and presented digitally these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.420
some notable exceptions continue to opt for&nbsp;
old-school film. Acclaimed filmmakers like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:02.280
Quentin Tarantino, Martin Scorsese, and&nbsp;
Christopher Nolan all eschew digital in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.360
favor of film. According to an interview&nbsp;
with Cinemablend, Tarantino claimed,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.400
"If you're not doing the work to get a great shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.660
then what's the point? Digital cinematography&nbsp;
puts all the heavy work in the post-production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.583
rather than planning and attempting&nbsp;
to get the perfect shot while on set."

00:06:16.583 --> 00:06:21.960
Fukunaga and cinematographer Adam Arkapaw&nbsp;
chose to shoot on 35mm film for all eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.320
episodes of Season 1, which made it&nbsp;
much more difficult to get some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.860
the more complex shots in the show.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, it looks like this may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.663
exclude that famous six-minute tracking&nbsp;
shot in the episode "Who Goes There?"

00:06:32.663 --> 00:06:35.270
"Come out with your hands raised!"

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.542
"Get down on your knees!"

00:06:37.542 --> 00:06:39.062
During an interview with Variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.143 --> 00:06:42.441
Arkapaw touched on the challenge of&nbsp;
filming a show this way. He said,

00:06:42.542 --> 00:06:46.697
"In a film, you might be accustomed to shooting&nbsp;
two to three minutes of screen time a day. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.697 --> 00:06:50.580
True Detective, we were shooting five&nbsp;
to six minutes every day. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.303
your time to devise, prepare and&nbsp;
execute your work is cut in half."

00:06:54.303 --> 00:06:57.060
Many fans and critics agree that&nbsp;
the extra effort was worth it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.800
with Season 1's visual richness being a big&nbsp;
part of its overall appeal. Sadly — according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.923
to PremiumBeat — subsequent seasons&nbsp;
weren't so lucky and were shot digitally.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.740
The second season of True Detective brought&nbsp;
in an entirely new cast of characters in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:15.660
completely different location, with arguably mixed&nbsp;
results. While critics didn't seem to appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.040
the supposed decline in writing and story, they&nbsp;
acknowledged the strong leading performances of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:25.380
the many cops and crooks operating in Los Angeles.&nbsp;
This included Rachel McAdams as Ani Bezzerides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.360
Vince Vaughn as Frank Semyon, and Taylor Kitsch&nbsp;
as Paul Woodrugh. But the actor who seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.380
make the most waves among the cast was&nbsp;
Colin Farrell as Ray Velcoro — a dirty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.881
cop torn between his desire to do the&nbsp;
right thing and his criminal loyalties.

00:07:37.881 --> 00:07:39.561
"I remember you dumping a body."

00:07:39.561 --> 00:07:45.782
"The reasons for all this, all that,&nbsp;
might not exist for me anymore."

00:07:45.782 --> 00:07:49.740
Farrell has had a long and eclectic career,&nbsp;
playing a wide array of characters in movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:54.120
like In Bruges, Fantastic Beasts and Where&nbsp;
to Find Them, and The Batman. At the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.780
he was added to the True Detective cast, he&nbsp;
was most often known for playing comedic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.842
charming rogues. But after being cast&nbsp;
as the alcoholic, deadbeat detective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.842 --> 00:08:06.060
Farrell had to change his body to match.&nbsp;
According to ET, the actor gained roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.620
20 pounds prior to filming and hated&nbsp;
every second of it. Farrell claimed,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.620
"People think it must be fun to&nbsp;
eat what you want — and it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.960
for the first day {...} Day&nbsp;
two, when you're already on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:20.322
your second cheeseburger and fries with a&nbsp;
chocolate shake by 11 a.m., not so much."

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.060
Given the success of Season 1, many&nbsp;
thought that Pizzolatto's writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.300
and Fukunaga's directing were a&nbsp;
match made in heaven. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.401
the pair reportedly didn't get along well&nbsp;
during production. In the past few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.401 --> 00:08:35.580
Fukunaga has gone on record explaining how the&nbsp;
working partnership between him and Pizzolatto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:40.200
deteriorated as the show went on. During an&nbsp;
interview with The Hollywood Reporter, he claimed,

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:41.400
"Over the course of the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.420
Nic kept positioning himself as if he was&nbsp;
my boss and I was like, 'But you're not my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.200
boss. We're partners. We collaborate.'&nbsp;
By the time they got to postproduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.423
people like [former HBO programming president]&nbsp;
Michael Lombardo were giving Nic more power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.423 --> 00:08:57.742
It was disheartening because it didn't&nbsp;
feel like the partnership was fair."

00:08:57.742 --> 00:09:01.620
It seems that this tension was one of the&nbsp;
reasons Fukunaga did not return for season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:07.020
two or three of True Detective, though he did&nbsp;
retain his role and credit as executive producer.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.940
Some claim that one of the unsung heroes of&nbsp;
True Detective is actress Alexandra Daddario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.880
as the character Lisa, a court stenographer and&nbsp;
Marty Hart's mistress in 1995. While Lisa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:22.680
Marty's secret affair becomes increasingly&nbsp;
toxic, Daddario brings genuine empathy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:27.864
complexity, and depth to a character who easily&nbsp;
could have been written off as superficial.

00:09:27.864 --> 00:09:29.822
"Go away. I'll call the cops."

00:09:29.822 --> 00:09:32.467
"Well, let's see how that works out for you."

00:09:32.467 --> 00:09:33.655
"I know other cops."

00:09:33.655 --> 00:09:33.994
"OK."

00:09:33.994 --> 00:09:34.560
"Get out!"

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.440
However, her character being highly sexualized led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.024
to Daddario's apprehension about filming&nbsp;
her first-ever nude scenes for the show.

00:09:42.024 --> 00:09:44.700
According to a 2014 interview with MTV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.883
the actress explained just how difficult&nbsp;
it was to prepare herself for her scene,

00:09:48.883 --> 00:09:52.500
"I really wanted to be part of the show,&nbsp;
and I understood why the nudity and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.380
of that was required of the character&nbsp;
{....} The character is really different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.740
from anything that I've done before.&nbsp;
The nudity was just part of that."

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.460
Fortunately, Daddario claimed that the&nbsp;
cast and crew helped put her at ease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:06.683
with Harrelson in particular making the&nbsp;
whole interaction very comfortable for her.

00:10:06.683 --> 00:10:09.900
Although it's hard now to imagine&nbsp;
Rust Cohle played by anyone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:14.580
it turns out that the casting was originally going&nbsp;
to be flipped, with McConaughey slated to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.420
Marty instead. At this time, McConaughey&nbsp;
was mainly known for his comedic roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.200
going all the way back to his breakthrough&nbsp;
role in Richard Linklater's Dazed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.260
Confused. While he'd already branched&nbsp;
further out with more serious roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:30.000
he was still seen as a jovial performer who would&nbsp;
fit better as the more light-hearted Marty Hart.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.020
During an interview with WNYC in&nbsp;
2014, McConaughey talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:38.640
his experience being brought in by HBO to read&nbsp;
for Marty. While he was intrigued by the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.182
it was actually Rust Cohle's dialogue&nbsp;
that piqued his interest. He said,

00:10:42.182 --> 00:10:44.220
"The guy I can't wait to hear what comes out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.342
his mouth every time I turn the&nbsp;
page is this guy Rustin Cohle."

00:10:47.342 --> 00:10:50.880
Once McConaughey realized how badly he&nbsp;
wanted to play Rust instead of Marty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.520
he apparently laid it out&nbsp;
to the showrunners, saying,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.536
"I read the first two episodes and said, 'Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.536 --> 00:11:00.000
I like the role of Cohle. If you guys are&nbsp;
interested in me for the role, I'm in.'"

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.900
He was ultimately right, considering his&nbsp;
performance in True Detective received some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:08.323
of the highest praise he's ever gotten from&nbsp;
audiences and critics throughout his career.

